NNAAMMEE
     llpp — front-end to the print spooler

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llpp [--ccss] [--oo _o_p_t_i_o_n] [--dd _p_r_i_n_t_e_r] [--nn _n_u_m] [_n_a_m_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llpp utility is a front-end to the print spooler as required by the
     IEEE Std 1003.2 (“POSIX.2”) specification.  It effectively invokes lpr(1)
     with the proper set of arguments.

     It generally prints the named files on the destination printer.

     The following options are available:

     --cc      Make the llpp command exit only after further access to any of the
             input files is no longer required.  The application can then
             safely delete or modify the files without affecting the output
             operation.

     --dd _d_e_s_t
             Specify a particular printer.  If no --dd is provided on the com‐
             mand line, the contents of the environment variables LPDEST or
             PRINTER (with this precedence) are taken as the destination
             printer.

     --mm      Send mail upon completion.

     --nn _n_u_m  Specify that _n_u_m copies of each of the named files shall be
             printed.

     --oo _o_p_t_i_o_n
             Printer specific options.  Not supported, provided only as a com‐
             patibility option for SVR.

     --ss      Silent operation.  Not supported, provided only as a compatibil‐
             ity option for Version 2 of the Single UNIX Specification
             (“SUSv2”).

     --tt _t_i_t_l_e
             Set the job title to _t_i_t_l_e.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     As described above, the variables LPDEST and PRINTER are examined to se‐
     lect the destination printer.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     lpr(1)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The llpp command is expected to comply with the IEEE Std 1003.2 (“POSIX.2”)
     specification.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     This implementation of the llpp command has been written by Jörg Wunsch.

BBUUGGSS
     The IEEE Std 1003.2 (“POSIX.2”) specification does not provide any means
     to print non-text files.  It rather requires the files to be printed to
     be text files limited to reasonable line lengths and printable charac‐
     ters.

